            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> > * Verified that `nvim --embed --listen 0.0.0.0:8888 < /dev/null` no longer crashes and successfully waits for a client.\r\n> \r\n> Really? I think you are making things up.\r\n> \r\n> And where is the \"setting `chan->detach = true` on the stdio channel\" you mentioned in [#38456 (comment)](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/38456#issuecomment-4984973877)?\r\n\r\nDamn, you're right. I got my wires crossed from a different local patch I was messing with when I wrote the PR description.\r\n\r\nAs it is right now, the PR only stops a hard `abort()` if the stdio channel completely fails to open. But the user in the issue is using `< /dev/null`, which actually opens fine but hits an immediate EOF. The PR doesn't change how EOF is handled, so yeah, it doesn't actually fix their issue.\r\n\r\nI did initially try setting `chan->detach = true` on the stdio channel to make it survive the EOF (like I mentioned in the issue thread), but I had to drop it because it completely broke the functional tests. The test runner uses `--embed` and `--listen` for almost every child process it spins up, and heavily relies on the child exiting the second it closes the stdio pipes.\r\n\r\nDigging into that made me realize the behavior they reported isn't even a bug. `--embed` means the process is managed over stdio, so if stdio closes, it should exit. The fact that it stayed alive in 0.9.5 was actually a bug itself (it was just hanging forever waiting for a UI on a dead stream).\r\n\r\nIf someone wants a background server to connect to later without keeping stdio open, they should really just be using `--headless`.\r\n\r\nSo, uh, yeah, my whole attempt to fix this was basically based on a flawed assumption of how `--embed` should work when the stream drops. I'll go ahead and close this PR, and we should probably just close the issue as expected behavior. Thanks for catching my dumbassery 🙏",
            "created_at": "2026-07-16T02:28:13Z",
